**Addressing patch issues**

I executed the apply_patch command through the shell, and although I received a warning, the patch succeeded. I'll need to proceed, but it seems there was a suggestion to use the designated apply_patch tool. I created an untracked file, p002-test-output.log, in the repository, which should be removed, but before that, I must fix some tests. The object menu tap issue appears to be related to overlay positions, so I need to explore how the hit test is functioning.
**Analyzing menu item tap issues**

I suspect the first menu item might be clipped on the right side due to the popup at the toolbar's edge, yet it should still hit the ancestor. I think it's a good idea to update tests to use _popupMenuItemFinder('创建块副本') instead of just tapping the text since this helper targets the center of the PopupMenuItem directly. It seems our current tests rely on old methods that aren't robust. The menu items at the right edge might be an offset issue, possibly causing taps to be absorbed by an overlay, which needs addressing.
**Examining tap behavior and helpers**

The tester indicated that the command sends a tap, but it seems it didn’t hit the intended menu item. I think using the helper could resolve this. First, I need to rerun the named tests, but perhaps I should also run some smaller tests that use find.text directly for the code menu. The helper _popupMenuItemFinder specifically targets PopupMenuItem for the _ObjectMenuSelection, which is fine. I’ll use _popupMenuItemFinder('python') if the text is singular. I’m curious if await tester.tap(find.text('python').last) will trigger any warnings.
